1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,395
It's the third game.

2
00:00:01,395 --> 00:00:05,220
The shuttle drops short at the net,
and your legs just say no.

3
00:00:05,570 --> 00:00:07,251
Your opponent looks fresh.

4
00:00:07,251 --> 00:00:10,114
You're bent over, gasping
between every point.

5
00:00:10,464 --> 00:00:11,946
You'll still get tired.

6
00:00:11,946 --> 00:00:12,872
Everyone does.

7
00:00:12,872 --> 00:00:15,712
But you can push it much
later into the match.

8
00:00:16,062 --> 00:00:19,383
Badminton doesn't tire you out
the way running does.

9
00:00:19,383 --> 00:00:23,081
And once you see how it really
works, you can train for it.

10
00:00:23,431 --> 00:00:27,096
Today I'll show you the two
engines badminton runs on,

11
00:00:27,096 --> 00:00:29,362
and exactly how to build each one.

12
00:00:29,711 --> 00:00:31,760
A badminton rally is short.

13
00:00:31,760 --> 00:00:36,296
Even at the top level, a typical
rally lasts about 7 seconds.

14
00:00:36,296 --> 00:00:39,076
Then you get about 15 seconds of rest.

15
00:00:39,427 --> 00:00:40,250
Burst, rest.

16
00:00:40,250 --> 00:00:41,073
Burst, rest.

17
00:00:41,073 --> 00:00:43,288
Over and over, for the whole match.

18
00:00:43,638 --> 00:00:44,672
And it's hard.

19
00:00:44,672 --> 00:00:50,256
Across studies, players' heart rate averages
around 90% of their maximum for the

20
00:00:50,256 --> 00:00:51,083
whole match.

21
00:00:51,433 --> 00:00:57,328
Even club players in their thirties averaged
around 168 beats per minute in a

22
00:00:57,328 --> 00:00:58,537
30-minute match.

23
00:00:58,887 --> 00:01:01,148
So your body runs two main engines.

24
00:01:01,849 --> 00:01:04,098
Engine one is the sprint battery.

25
00:01:04,098 --> 00:01:07,801
It powers every lunge, jump and
push back to the middle.

26
00:01:08,151 --> 00:01:10,170
Engine two is the recharger.

27
00:01:10,170 --> 00:01:15,042
It's your aerobic system, and it refills
that battery between rallies.

28
00:01:15,391 --> 00:01:19,978
About two-thirds of the total energy
actually comes from the recharger.

29
00:01:20,328 --> 00:01:23,795
There's a third engine, the one
behind the leg burn.

30
00:01:23,795 --> 00:01:28,505
It's always running a little, but in
badminton it plays a smaller part.

31
00:01:28,505 --> 00:01:31,187
You feel it most in long, brutal rallies.

32
00:01:31,537 --> 00:01:34,049
Engine one is like a camera flash.

33
00:01:34,049 --> 00:01:38,641
Huge power, instantly, but only for a
few seconds at full blast.

34
00:01:38,991 --> 00:01:43,461
Think of a split step, a lunge to the net,
and a push back to the middle.

35
00:01:43,461 --> 00:01:45,455
That's the sprint battery firing.

36
00:01:45,805 --> 00:01:50,655
One study of 20 men's singles
matches counted, on average,

37
00:01:50,655 --> 00:01:54,765
about 46 full lunges and 52
half lunges per match.

38
00:01:55,115 --> 00:02:00,725
To train it, copy the rhythm of a real rally:
short, fast, and then real rest.

39
00:02:00,725 --> 00:02:05,270
Repeated short sprints are a proven
way to train racket players.

40
00:02:05,619 --> 00:02:06,923
Quick check first.

41
00:02:06,923 --> 00:02:12,274
If you've had chest pain, fainting or unusual
breathlessness when exercising,

42
00:02:12,274 --> 00:02:16,734
or you have a heart condition, see a
doctor before hard training.

43
00:02:17,084 --> 00:02:19,221
Drill one: shadow rally repeats.

44
00:02:19,221 --> 00:02:22,588
A good starting point, built
from real match rhythm:

45
00:02:22,938 --> 00:02:28,083
move to a corner and back to the middle at
full speed for 8 to 10 seconds,

46
00:02:28,083 --> 00:02:29,935
then rest 20 to 30 seconds.

47
00:02:30,285 --> 00:02:36,386
Try 8 reps, 2 or 3 sets, with 3 easy minutes
between sets, once or twice a week.

48
00:02:36,737 --> 00:02:39,132
Drill two: multi-shuttle feeding.

49
00:02:39,132 --> 00:02:43,287
A partner feeds 8 to 12 shuttles
fast to all four corners.

50
00:02:43,287 --> 00:02:46,316
Then you swap, so you rest
while they work.

51
00:02:46,665 --> 00:02:48,803
Two sets of 8 rounds is plenty.

52
00:02:48,803 --> 00:02:51,209
Stop when your footwork gets sloppy.

53
00:02:51,559 --> 00:02:53,699
Drill three: reaction starts.

54
00:02:53,699 --> 00:02:59,404
Split step on your partner's call, then
explode 2 or 3 steps to the corner they

55
00:02:59,404 --> 00:03:00,117
point at.

56
00:03:00,117 --> 00:03:03,612
5 or 6 reps, 3 sets, with full
rest between reps.

57
00:03:03,963 --> 00:03:09,505
Always warm up first: 5 minutes of easy
movement, then walking lunges, skips,

58
00:03:09,505 --> 00:03:12,560
and a few build-up sprints
and small jumps.

59
00:03:12,910 --> 00:03:14,425
New to hard training?

60
00:03:14,425 --> 00:03:18,490
Spend 3 to 4 weeks on the aerobic
and strength work first.

61
00:03:18,490 --> 00:03:21,934
Then start with 2 sets of 6
reps and longer rests.

62
00:03:22,284 --> 00:03:25,112
The common mistake is cutting
the rest short.

63
00:03:25,112 --> 00:03:28,739
Every rep gets slower, and it stops
being sprint training.

64
00:03:28,739 --> 00:03:31,628
That's a different workout,
which we'll get to.

65
00:03:31,978 --> 00:03:32,984
Quality first.

66
00:03:32,984 --> 00:03:35,263
If your speed drops, stop the set.

67
00:03:36,063 --> 00:03:38,269
Engine two is the phone charger.

68
00:03:38,269 --> 00:03:40,474
The battery gives you the burst.

69
00:03:40,474 --> 00:03:44,618
The charger decides how full it is
when the next rally starts.

70
00:03:44,968 --> 00:03:49,744
Fitter players tend to recover their power
faster between repeated sprints,

71
00:03:49,744 --> 00:03:53,011
and refilling that sprint fuel
is a big part of why.

72
00:03:53,361 --> 00:03:59,122
Researchers who tested international players in
a simulated match agreed: you need a

73
00:03:59,122 --> 00:04:02,918
strong aerobic engine for fast
recovery between rallies.

74
00:04:03,268 --> 00:04:07,513
With a weak charger, you start each rally a
little emptier than the last.

75
00:04:08,813 --> 00:04:11,101
You can even see it in club players.

76
00:04:11,101 --> 00:04:16,109
In the last 10 minutes of a match, heart rate
climbs and the effort feels harder.

77
00:04:16,459 --> 00:04:18,794
Drill one: easy aerobic base.

78
00:04:18,794 --> 00:04:24,787
Jog, cycle or swim at a pace where you can
still talk, for 30 to 45 minutes,

79
00:04:24,787 --> 00:04:26,422
once or twice a week.

80
00:04:26,772 --> 00:04:28,702
Drill two: long intervals.

81
00:04:28,702 --> 00:04:33,850
A good starting point: 4 rounds of 3 to 4
minutes, hard but controlled,

82
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:37,353
with 2 to 3 minutes easy in
between, once a week.

83
00:04:37,703 --> 00:04:41,325
Drill three: court blocks, a
good starting point.

84
00:04:41,325 --> 00:04:46,396
Shadow footwork for 15 seconds, rest 15
seconds, for 8 to 10 minutes.

85
00:04:46,396 --> 00:04:48,135
Strong, but not all out.

86
00:04:48,485 --> 00:04:54,378
Early studies show interval training can raise
aerobic fitness in badminton players

87
00:04:54,378 --> 00:04:56,272
within about 4 to 10 weeks.

88
00:04:57,672 --> 00:04:59,261
Build it up gradually.

89
00:04:59,261 --> 00:05:04,648
Add a little time or a little intensity, one
step at a time, not both at once.

90
00:05:04,998 --> 00:05:07,118
The mistake is picking only one.

91
00:05:07,118 --> 00:05:10,267
Only long jogs, and you never
train your sprints.

92
00:05:10,617 --> 00:05:14,756
Only hard intervals, and you'll
struggle to recover from them,

93
00:05:14,756 --> 00:05:18,764
because a solid aerobic base helps
you handle hard training.

94
00:05:18,764 --> 00:05:19,684
You need both.

95
00:05:20,584 --> 00:05:24,068
Your two engines need a chassis
that can take a beating.

96
00:05:24,068 --> 00:05:25,107
That's your legs.

97
00:05:25,457 --> 00:05:27,093
Think of car suspension.

98
00:05:27,093 --> 00:05:31,281
Strong shocks handle every lunge and
landing with room to spare.

99
00:05:31,631 --> 00:05:35,791
Singles players lunge and jump far
more than doubles players do.

100
00:05:36,141 --> 00:05:39,651
Strength training makes badminton
players more powerful,

101
00:05:39,651 --> 00:05:42,114
faster and better at changing direction.

102
00:05:42,464 --> 00:05:46,743
In a big review of injury-prevention
trials across many sports,

103
00:05:46,743 --> 00:05:49,952
strength training cut injuries
to about a third.

104
00:05:51,352 --> 00:05:55,363
That matters for one injury in
particular: the Achilles tendon.

105
00:05:55,713 --> 00:06:02,861
In one study, 97% of badminton Achilles
ruptures happened to recreational players.

106
00:06:02,861 --> 00:06:05,014
Their average age was 36.

107
00:06:05,364 --> 00:06:09,623
And 94% happened in the middle or
at the end of a game.

108
00:06:09,623 --> 00:06:13,577
The researchers think tired
muscles may play a part.

109
00:06:13,927 --> 00:06:17,484
Drill one: split squats,
building up to lunges.

110
00:06:17,484 --> 00:06:20,818
2 to 3 sets of 8 to 12 per
leg, twice a week.

111
00:06:21,168 --> 00:06:25,558
Drill two: calf raises, with
straight knees and bent knees.

112
00:06:25,558 --> 00:06:28,557
3 sets of 12 to 15, slow and controlled.

113
00:06:28,557 --> 00:06:31,557
Move from two legs to one when it's easy.

114
00:06:31,907 --> 00:06:36,917
Drill three: small hops, like pogo hops
and side-to-side line hops.

115
00:06:36,917 --> 00:06:42,296
Add them once the calf raises feel easy and
pain-free, after a few weeks.

116
00:06:42,647 --> 00:06:48,568
Start at the low end, around 60 easy contacts,
twice a week with 2 days between.

117
00:06:48,568 --> 00:06:53,612
Do them after your warm-up, on a sprung
court or grass, not concrete.

118
00:06:53,962 --> 00:06:57,056
Land softly and quietly, and
build up slowly.

119
00:06:57,056 --> 00:07:00,554
If your Achilles is sore the
next morning, back off.

120
00:07:00,904 --> 00:07:05,858
A landing tip from one small lab study:
after a rear-court forehand jump,

121
00:07:05,858 --> 00:07:10,209
rotate your upper body so your
non-racket foot lands turned out,

122
00:07:10,209 --> 00:07:12,083
like the pros' scissor kick.

123
00:07:12,433 --> 00:07:16,763
That cut the estimated load on the
Achilles by about a quarter.

124
00:07:16,763 --> 00:07:19,942
Practise it slowly first,
ideally with a coach.

125
00:07:20,292 --> 00:07:21,551
The common mistake?

126
00:07:21,551 --> 00:07:23,881
Stretching instead of strengthening.

127
00:07:23,881 --> 00:07:26,651
Stretching programs didn't
reduce injuries.

128
00:07:26,651 --> 00:07:28,036
Strength training did.

129
00:07:28,386 --> 00:07:31,650
If your heel or calf hurts,
don't play through it.

130
00:07:31,650 --> 00:07:33,378
Get it checked by a physio.

131
00:07:33,728 --> 00:07:37,875
And if you ever feel a sudden snap or a
kick to the back of your heel,

132
00:07:37,875 --> 00:07:41,088
stop playing straight away and get
urgent medical care.

133
00:07:41,438 --> 00:07:43,977
Rest between rallies isn't dead time.

134
00:07:43,977 --> 00:07:46,515
It's where the recharger does its job.

135
00:07:46,865 --> 00:07:52,281
In Olympic finals, the rest between rallies
more than doubled over 20 years,

136
00:07:52,281 --> 00:07:54,673
from about 15 to over 30 seconds.

137
00:07:54,673 --> 00:07:59,878
The researchers think players needed more
recovery as the game got faster.

138
00:08:00,229 --> 00:08:02,925
Habit one: use the time between rallies.

139
00:08:02,925 --> 00:08:07,726
Walk back, breathe, reset, and don't rush
your serve when you're gassed.

140
00:08:07,726 --> 00:08:11,408
Just don't stall: the rules say
play must be continuous.

141
00:08:11,757 --> 00:08:13,864
Habit two: drink in the breaks.

142
00:08:13,864 --> 00:08:17,090
Players can lose around a litre
of sweat an hour.

143
00:08:17,441 --> 00:08:22,236
Losing more than 2 to 3% of your body
weight in fluid hurts performance.

144
00:08:22,236 --> 00:08:24,929
But don't force down more than you sweat.

145
00:08:25,279 --> 00:08:30,654
Habit three: for long sessions over an hour,
like tournaments, have some carbs.

146
00:08:30,654 --> 00:08:32,468
A sports drink or a banana.

147
00:08:33,368 --> 00:08:34,864
Here's a simple week.

148
00:08:34,864 --> 00:08:40,032
Two strength days, at least 2 days apart,
with your calf raises and, later,

149
00:08:40,032 --> 00:08:40,780
your hops.

150
00:08:40,780 --> 00:08:44,248
One easy aerobic session, and
one interval session.

151
00:08:44,598 --> 00:08:48,546
One or two short sets of rally
repeats before one club night,

152
00:08:48,546 --> 00:08:50,075
after a proper warm-up.

153
00:08:50,075 --> 00:08:53,195
Play your club games, and keep
one full rest day.

154
00:08:53,545 --> 00:08:55,619
Don't start everything at once.

155
00:08:55,619 --> 00:09:00,869
Add one new session every week or two, keep
your hard days apart, and skip hops,

156
00:09:00,869 --> 00:09:03,785
sprints and intervals the day
before a match.

157
00:09:04,135 --> 00:09:08,444
If you're sore or flat, swap a hard
session for an easy one.

158
00:09:08,444 --> 00:09:09,150
Under 18?

159
00:09:09,150 --> 00:09:13,671
Learn the strength and jump drills
with a qualified coach first.

160
00:09:14,021 --> 00:09:15,564
Give it 4 to 10 weeks.

161
00:09:15,564 --> 00:09:19,120
That's how long training studies
took to see changes.

162
00:09:19,470 --> 00:09:21,495
A small battery that bursts.

163
00:09:21,495 --> 00:09:23,659
A big charger that refills it.

164
00:09:23,659 --> 00:09:25,614
Strong legs that carry both.

165
00:09:25,964 --> 00:09:28,749
Train those, use the gaps, and
in the third game,

166
00:09:28,749 --> 00:09:30,977
you'll be the one who still looks fresh.
